# Planner Task Schema — Sheni type layer, Convention v0.1.0
#
# Each key under `fields` names one field of a task document and the Sheni
# type it is read at. The types are the ones the `sheni_types` crate ships;
# nothing here is invented for the example.
#
# The rule that shapes this file is ADR sheni_03: a field may be `optional`
# only where its type has a fallback, and a fallback must be a member of the
# value space meaning "not known" -- never a legal value borrowed to stand in
# for one. So `priority` is required because `u8` has no honest fallback,
# while `assignee_count` is optional because `soft_u8` does.

schema:
  name: planner-task
  version: 0.1.0
  description: |
    A task in a planner, written to show what the soft types are for. Every
    optional field below falls back to a value that says "nobody knows",
    which is the difference between an unscheduled task and a task scheduled
    for the epoch.

fields:

  # -- Identity ------------------------------------------------------------

  id:
    type: uuid
    required: required
    description: RFC 9562 UUID, canonical hyphenated form.

  title:
    type: string
    required: required
    description: One line naming the task.

  # -- Scheduling ----------------------------------------------------------
  # This is what the soft types exist for. A planner that models an unknown
  # due date as a null cannot tell "not scheduled" from "known to the quarter".

  due:
    type: soft_date
    required: optional
    fallback: XXXX
    description: |
      When the task is due, to whatever precision is actually known --
      a day, a month, a quarter (`2026-35` is Q3), a year, or nothing.
      Falls back to the fully unspecified date rather than to an epoch.

  window:
    type: soft_date_range
    required: optional
    fallback: XXXX/XXXX
    description: |
      The span the task must land inside. Either end may be a coarse date,
      open (`..`, the range genuinely has no end), or unknown (empty, it has
      an end nobody recorded). Those are different claims and stay apart.

  estimate:
    type: duration_iso
    required: required
    description: |
      ISO 8601 period. Required, and deliberately: `duration_iso` has no
      fallback, so there is no honest way to leave it out. A duration of zero
      is an estimate somebody made.

  # -- State ---------------------------------------------------------------

  priority:
    type: u8
    required: required
    description: |
      Required for the same reason. Zero is a priority, not an absence, so
      `u8` has no fallback and an optional priority is not offered.

  assignee_count:
    type: soft_u8
    required: optional
    fallback: unknown
    description: |
      How many people are on it. Soft, because a count of zero is a fact
      somebody established and "nobody has looked" is a different fact.

  blocked:
    type: soft_bool
    required: optional
    fallback: unknown
    description: |
      Whether the task is blocked. Soft, because a flag reading `false`
      because nobody set it is indistinguishable from one deliberately
      turned off. Carries Kleene's `and`, `or` and `not`.
